1
00:00:01,843 --> 00:00:05,342
Somewhere, right now, someone is recording encrypted traffic.

2
00:00:06,527 --> 00:00:09,792
Card payments. Medical records. Private messages.

3
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:13,240
They can't read any of it. Not yet.

4
00:00:16,004 --> 00:00:26,082
When a large enough quantum computer exists, the math behind R S A and elliptic curves stops protecting anything. Data taken today gets opened then.

5
00:00:27,004 --> 00:00:34,431
NIST published the post-quantum standards in twenty twenty-four, and has proposed retiring the old algorithms by twenty thirty.

6
00:00:36,142 --> 00:00:48,852
Michele Mosca put the risk in one line. If the years your data must stay secret, plus the years it takes to migrate, add up to more than the years until that computer arrives... you are already exposed.

7
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:57,693
So every team has to answer three questions. Where is our cryptography? What matters most? And who fixes it?

8
00:01:00,589 --> 00:01:05,107
This is Mosca. A quantum-readiness teammate that works inside your team chat.

9
00:01:08,398 --> 00:01:18,349
In a Switch room, someone asks it to scan. Mosca reads the code, the configs, the certificates, and ranks every algorithm by its probability of exposure.

10
00:01:19,271 --> 00:01:25,616
It attaches a standard cryptographic bill of materials to the room, so everyone works from the same inventory.

11
00:01:27,195 --> 00:01:32,476
Then it asks the one question only a person can answer. How long must this stay trusted?

12
00:01:33,661 --> 00:01:43,418
The owner says ten years. Mosca records the answer, with a name, in an append-only ledger. Two signatures jump from one percent to seventy. Overdue.

13
00:01:45,656 --> 00:01:57,809
Now, the fix. Claude Code holds the room's migrator role. One agent at a time. It moves the edge to hybrid post-quantum key exchange on a branch, and says plainly what it could not test.

14
00:01:58,862 --> 00:02:03,698
Mosca rescans the branch. Triple DES is gone. Exposure is down.

15
00:02:05,409 --> 00:02:12,921
OpenCode, a second agent from a different vendor, holds the reviewer role. It checks the change line by line, and approves.

16
00:02:13,974 --> 00:02:19,430
A person signs off. And the migrator declines to merge, because its role says people merge.

17
00:02:21,668 --> 00:02:27,086
Every answer and every assessment is kept. So you can always show who decided what, and when.

18
00:02:28,139 --> 00:02:35,924
The model is open. The assumptions are yours to change. And every number you just saw came from a real session, not a slide.

19
00:02:38,821 --> 00:02:41,581
The data you encrypt today has a decryption date.

20
00:02:43,424 --> 00:02:46,102
Mosca helps your team find it, and move first.
